# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: dxf-writer-header-not-utf8 — an AC1021+ ASCII DXF written by clautolisp holds ISO-8859-1 bytes
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P3
#+TYPE: bug
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-dxf-writer-header-not-utf8
#+DATE: 2026-10-23

* Symptom

Found by the dxf-angles probe's clautolisp baseline run
(=scripts/run-dxf-angles-probe.sh --backend clautolisp=, 2026-10-23): the
DXF cador writes through vla-SaveAs (=$ACADVER AC1027=) contains the byte
#xB5 (MICRO SIGN in ISO-8859-1) three times, in the header values of
=$AREAUNITS=, =$LENGTHUNITS= and =$VOLUMEUNITS= ("in ft mi µm mm cm m km").
A DXF of version AC1021 (2007) or later is UTF-8, where #xB5 alone is not a
character; clautolisp's own AutoLISP =read-line= (UTF-8 by default) stops
with "UTF-8 stream decoding error ... #(181)" on that file, and a vendor
reading it would see an invalid sequence.

* Cause

=+dxf-external-format+= (=clautolisp/drawing/source/dxf.lisp=) is
=:iso-8859-1= for both reading and writing, by design ("Honouring
$DWGCODEPAGE precisely is a future refinement"). Reading as ISO-8859-1 is
harmless (bytes round-trip); WRITING non-ASCII text that way is wrong for
AC1021+.

* Proposal

Write AC1021+ ASCII DXF as UTF-8 (and read it as UTF-8 with a fallback),
or keep ISO-8859-1 only for pre-2007 versions with \U+ escapes for
characters outside $DWGCODEPAGE. Check those three header variables'
values against a vendor-written DXF (the dxf-angles probe's DXF in
=dist/dxf-angles/= is one).


Resolved (2026-10-25, clautolisp 2.3.64)
----------------------------------------

The proposal's two halves, in clautolisp/drawing/source/dxf.lisp:
- Writing: an ASCII DXF of AC1021 or later is written as UTF-8; an earlier
  one as ISO-8859-1, a character above #xFF as \U+XXXX, and its
  $DWGCODEPAGE as ANSI_1252 (the session's "UTF-8" would be false there).
- Reading: $ACADVER is sniffed from the header; AC1021+ is read as UTF-8,
  falling back to ISO-8859-1 on a file that is not valid UTF-8 (such as the
  ones clautolisp wrote before); an earlier file as ISO-8859-1 with its
  \U+XXXX escapes decoded.
Binary DXF strings stay Latin-1 bytes (the binary codec's documented
scope).

Tests DXF-AC1021-AND-LATER-ARE-WRITTEN-AND-READ-AS-UTF-8,
DXF-BEFORE-AC1021-ESCAPES-WHAT-LATIN-1-CANNOT-HOLD,
DXF-UTF-8-VERSION-FALLS-BACK-TO-LATIN-1-ON-INVALID-BYTES. The repro
(run-dxf-angles-probe.sh --backend clautolisp) now writes an AC1027 file
that is valid UTF-8, the MICRO SIGN as C2 B5.

The check against vendor DXF (dxf-angles: AutoCAD 2022, BricsCAD V25
Windows / V26 macOS, all AC1032): none writes $AREAUNITS, $LENGTHUNITS or
$VOLUMEUNITS -- they are BricsCAD sysvars, not DXF header variables -- and
all write $DWGCODEPAGE with code 3, ANSI_1252. clautolisp writes every
sysvar of the drawing into the header ($LENGTHUNITS even with its quotes):
issues/open/deferred-dxf-header-only-header-variables.issue.
